Subject: OSF/1 3.0 and slattach
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96075321606548



Hello,

I am running OSF/1 3.0 on a DECpc 150 AXP system and I have a slip 
connection to the internet at 38400 baud. Everything works OK except that 
I get the following message in the console log

acerint: overrun error; status = 23
ace0: input silo overflow
acerint: overrun error; status = 63
ace0: input silo overflow


These messages occur when a large amount of data is being sent to my 
system. For example, if I ftp a file from my Alpha to another system the 
error does not come up but if I ftp a file from a remote site to my 
system the error comes up often.

Most of the time everything works but once in a while the slip connection 
fails after reporting the error:


Any ideas?

Thank you in advance



David Halyk
CashHandler Retail Systems Inc
(New home of the DECpos Point of Sale Systems)

From tru64-unix-managers  Fri Jul 14 21:31:03 1995
From: Michael Matthews <matthewm () sgate ! com>
Date: Fri, 14 Jul 1995 21:31:03 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: Questions about Alpha InternetServer 4/100
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96075321606547

I'm stumped, yet again.

First question.  Or more of a confirmation.  While you can enable the VGA
port on the motherboard by going into the ECU, enabling the VGA controller,
booting genvmunix and doing a rebuild, all you get is 640x480.  The VGA
controller on the motherboard is incapable of doing any better.  Is this
true?  If so, I'm really wondering why the #%^#^% DEC even bothered to put
such pathetic technology on the motherboard.  [I'm not in the best of moods
now, but I'll try to be as polite as possible.....]

Second.  There's a modem in these machines, comes standard.  As far as I can
tell, it's not configured.  Does not show up in ECU.  I try to add it, using
the generic OSF modem definition.

It gets hung on asking for the system config disk. WHAT THE BLAZES DOES IT
WANT???  I made a copy of the ECU and even went and did a "dry" save of the
config on it.  But it just won't accept anything here.  So.  How do you make
a system config disk?

I've got far better things to do on a Friday afternoon.  Like drive home in
traffic.

